<div class="mw-parser-output"><h1>Valhalla History</h1>
<p><i>The Eighth Manuscript of History</i>
</p><p><i>As scribed by Phalo, Scribe to the Paladins</i>
</p><p>Well known Valhalla folklore tells of heroes whose acts of bravery surpassed even that of the Great King Cromwell. But alas, even the innocent school children of New Midgaard learn little of their heritage or history, except of these legends which are passed from father to son. It saddens me to report that these legends may well have died in the hearts of those that most deserve remembering. Excuse me if you will, but I feel a bit of an explanation is required before you continue your quest for the knowledge of Valhalla's past... you see, but little recorded history remains today for mortal eyes to gaze upon. Why, it amazes even us humans that the manuscript held in constant guard by the Midgaard Paladins survived to this day at all. For my trade is that of a scribe as was my father and his father before him. It is believed that my ancestry actually dates to that of scribe to King Cromwell himself, although of course no proof can be offered of such. A search was begun by my father's father, Alainer of Udgaard in the year 132 of the Healing. His accepted quest was to make truth or yarn of the existence of the seven manuscripts, which were believed to record the history of Valhalla. His appointment by King Vincent the II of New Midgaard was that which encompassed a contract of faith and friendship to the King, and was said to have been more binding than that of pen or sword. My family's perseverance and dedication to the quest for our history is evidence of this faith and remains steadfast to this day.
</p><p>As luck would have it the Brion manuscript was found only three years after his quest began and soon after delivered to the hands of the Paladins themselves. It is believed to be factual and present day proof verifies the accounts as scribed. Luck however, was not repeated in the days that followed the discovery of this marvelous document. (Of note at this point, is that the third manuscript under Paladin guard is believed to be incomplete, for reasons you will soon learn).
</p><p>What you see before you now is written in the common tongue and decipherable by most of this land's races. It is but a short summation of years of personal journals, my own included. I'm afraid other written word of these accounts is extremely scarce. It should also be noted that most of the information uncovered and scribed to my family's journals began as mere rumors and tales, many of which were passed down from generation to generation. Hundreds of interviews have been conducted throughout the years and these findings written to our journals by quill as official record. I have included here much of what may have only been tales to begin with, but has since been verified by means of comparison with what little has been found remaining of the toils of the writers, craftsmen and builders who walked Valhalla before this age. Much of what I hold on record is mere speculation and yarns, and I have chosen not obscure fact at this time with unverifiable fiction.
</p><p>You see, the dragon Ancalador's power over these lands was absolute in the days before the reign of King Cromwell. Ancalador and his followers boasted no challenge by those without wings or other races of knowledge that walked on two legs. Travel to even the nearest of villages or abodes was a journey not to be taken lightly. For even today's well known path between East Udgaard Tower and the gates of Old Midgaard was lightly traveled and only the hearties of adventurers dared venture far from their presumed safety of home. Trade was virtually nonexistent, and even simple crafts and techniques remained guarded secrets, as trust between races was hard found. Ancalador and Tiamat held little concern of threat by mortals of which they considered pwerless against their forces. After all, the dragons knowingly being the oldest of Valhalla's creatures also luckily considered themselves the wisest. And how, in the mind of a dragon, could something so puny which couldn't even fly be considered more than a mere nuisance. For they knew even the Gods had afforded little time, energy or inclination to interfere in such trivial matters as what the dragons viewed as puny mortal affairs.
</p><p>But interfere the gods did, and although much of the story is hearsay and legend, even the simplest of ogres know there's truth in the tale. It seems by what records I have gathered that the Gods Odin and Palio held little love of the overbearing dragonkind, and saw this as a justifiable means to warrant their involvement in Valhalla's creatures affairs. Ancalador knew little of the great Gods favoritism of King Cromwell, and was caught off guard by Sir Gehron's final valiant charge during the Kings diversion. The powers the Gods bestowed on the army of mortals not only afforded a means to defeat the mighty dragons on that triumphant day, but provided a flame which still burns as magic and faith found in both the hearts and swords in all of the races of Valhalla. Although no proof was found of the demise of Ancalador or Tiamat, it is said that both were severely wounded and Ancalador was least seen heading toward the barren lands near Danalek with his tail between his legs. Within a fortnight news had traveled across the lands that the Gods had given aid to the races of Valhalla and there now existed a common force that could hold the evil forces of Tiamat and Ancalandor and their army of dark followers at bay. It is common knowledge that the celebration that followed lasted a full year of moons, and that even the dwarves who seldom stepped above ground and rarely socialized, showed a keen taste for the Human mead and Halfling Ale and joined in the rejoicing. The dragon's dark followers had little stomach for anything resembling a fair fight, and as you may well know, most slithered or crawled into the underground lands to exert their powers on those creatures without the means nor will to resist. Ah, and no doubt you're also well aware, (at least if you've traveled far enough to read this scribe's record), of the dangers that many areas still hold for the ill equipped or unprepared adventurer.
</p><p>As years passed following the defeat of Ancalador and Tiamat changes came slowly and at no small expense of life by those whose goals were to better the known lands of Valhalla. The first major feat (and no small feat it was) was the rebuilding of New Midgaard in its present day locale. And, although the New Midgaard walls have as of this writing never received an arrow in battle, the newly sworn boy King's council, urged the walls construction and felt that the offered protection was worth the six years effort to build. Next came the building of the new road and the great ships in the port town of Torsbay. The new King realized the need for growth and appointed teams of sea and land scouts empowered to search the lands and seas for trade routes and knowledge. In the first fifteen years alone, the discovery of Xiyang and Khorsabad by sea to the southeast, land routes from the halfling villages to the northwest, and river paths to Nymgaard opened doors for commerce, trade and travel. These previously uncharted routes quickly brought previously unknown races together. Why, some had never before set eyes on each other and to say there was a bit of distrust among them would be an understatement of facts. As time has passed, the walls which divide the races has eroded slowly. Even still, there is distrust between many. It is well established by what records exist that goblin raids on the gnome mining projects east of Urland were made in as early as the 150th Year of the Healing. For the newfound knowledge brought greed as well as charity. The King faced many such unsurmountable problems and made no efforts to eliminate hostilities between races. It is a sad fact to report and although I found no answer nor offer a solution to the dilemma, hopefully the day will come when even the wariest of elves will join hands with a orc in friendship, but then again, that may be asking a bit too much. Anyway, soon after the first trade routes were discovered, it seems a bit of a revolution of sorts occurred. Oh no,not the kind of revolution which is necessarily bad, but a revolution of learning! By the Year 155 it is well documented that even those guilds which had been believed to be myth, (as most were believed to exist only in the minds of a wary adventurer after one too many pints of ale), began to surface. Before this time, only the word of a trusted member and a majority vote would gain a young prospect admission. Why, after this surfacing of the guilds, not only were they quite overt about their practices, but some even ran membership campaigns and pronounced their benefits above the next guild in an effort to bring "newbies" as they were know to their doors. Guilds flourished, and even considering the present day's somewhat inflated instructor fees, you'd be hard pressed to find a youth of consenting age not committed to their learnings.
</p><p>It was found documented that about the same period as this "guild revolution" was in full swing, an oddity of sorts began, which is known to be widely practiced as of this scribe. It seems that many young adventurers realized that even the strongest among them held little chance of a successful return to mother if they wandered off exploring the darkest reaches of Valhalla alone. Those who befriended each other and shared beliefs, banded together in organized groups. Well, even a dimwitted sewer lurker knows that there lays a certain degree of safety in numbers, and these bands of young adventurers teamed up and joined forces in what they called "clans". By Year 168 it seems that a number of these "clans" had formed, some even boasting as many as dozen or more members. Of these, some were (and still are as of this writing) quite overt in their activities, and seemed to be a fairly positive influence on the lands of Valhalla. Beware however, that factual record exists that there are those which did, and sill do not, hold the good of Valhalla as a premise of conduct. Documented accounts of hostile attacks by these clans on good citizens and on other clans have been made. These destructive clans remain deeply rooted in an underground system of sorts and the laws of the greater cities and communities have had little if any effect on their elimination.
</p><p>As you might have noticed I have labeled this manuscript with the number eight. Although the quest of my father's father, which befell upon me, has brought insight into much of the history of Valhalla, I realize that what is provided is sketchy at best. I have chosen eight because our endeavors have established that seven other manuscripts do in fact exist... or at least were known to have been scribed by others before our days and as of yet neither proof nor tale has been found verifying their destruction. Of course you're well aware that the third manuscript has been found, but although many leads were followed, (specifically for manuscripts five and six) none have proved fruitful. It is rumored that these manuscripts may have fallen into the hands of those who do not have Valhalla's future success in mind. And, although it has not been officially documented, it is believed that the information found in each of these seven manuscripts offer further explanation regarding the use of magic and powers by form of guidance from the mighty gods themselves. It should be offered as further substance of this most important discovery that, Bessmire, (daughter of Holkraf, (and direct descendent of Brion who recited the third manuscript) was told when but a child (by her father), of a page which was dictated by Brion at the direction of the God Palio herself. This page is said to be the final page of the third transcript. No reason exists to doubt her word and her family's reputation remains true. Information also has been unearthed collaborating this account which suggests the same regarding each of the seven manuscripts. If this is true, (and I believe it to be so) the very future of all of Valhalla may weigh upon these unfound documents. For, in the wrong hands they could surely change the course of Valhalla's future history for the worse.
</p><p>As my candle grows short, I wish to close these humble writings with word that my ventures throughout Valhalla have left me with a deep respect for the creatures both good and evil which call these great lands and waters their home. For the enduring will of survival is strong in both. Because of this do not estimate your enemies to be weak for it may prove your demise. Nor should you turn your back on those who would welcome your strengths, for we must stand together. My concerns are those shared by all who wish good upon the lands of Valhalla and although it seems that we of good heart hold the reins of power in this great land, do not underestimate the fragility of our existence or grow complacent as we venture forth into unknown lands. Although we have traveled great distances, we know not the extent of our coastlines or what creatures, be them friend or foe we will meet on tomorrow's path. I hope that good will prevail, but alas, even now in these seemingly civilized days, the laws of our townships do not reach beyond the light cast by the torches on the city walls. There are those who dwell in the darkness which await our mistakes, and would feel no compassion in our pain, no sadness or sorrow in our deaths. Take heed adventurer, for the history books and legends which tell of Valhalla's future heroes await you.
</p><p>Phalo, Scribe to the Paladins (Day 3, Month of the Heat, Year 281)
